            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Aha, thanks for the follow up!\n\nI think the solution might be to force `lua` parser to come from 'nvim-treesitter' and not from the one bundled with Neovim. Maybe OpenSUSE provides a parser that is not compatible with 'nvim-treesitter' queries.\n\nCould you try doing the following:\n- Using your system package manager, install `tree-sitter-cli` executable. It is a [requirement](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter/tree/main?tab=readme-ov-file#requirements) for 'nvim-treesitter'. I think [this](https://software.opensuse.org/package/tree-sitter) might be the one, but it looks like 0.25.3 version while 'nvim-treesitter' lists 0.26.1 as minimum version. Ideally, try to install the necessary version.\n- When in MiniMax, execute `:lua require('nvim-treesitter').install({ 'lua' })` and restart Neovim.\n- Now executing `:checkhealth vim.treesitter` should show that the `lua` parser is not `/usr/share/nvim/runtime/parser/lua.so`, but something like `/home/user/.local/share/nvim-minimax/site/parser/lua.so`.\n- Try opening a Lua file and hopefully there shouldn't be any errors.\n\nPlease, describe later how this went so that it can be documented in MiniMax itself.",
